/* ══ R77 · THE SQUARE DIAMOND ═════════════════════════════════════════════
   Same object language as Mr.Mah, same evidence. Scanning straight across the
   reference panel's frame, outside-in, gives a real extruded rail rather than
   a drawn outline:

     bright outer bevel face .......... ~17% of the frame, L≈200 (near white)
     falloff shoulder ................. ~7%
     main rail face ................... ~52%, L≈25
     inner catch line ................. ~2%,  L≈53
     near-black seat .................. ~19%, L≈5
     inner catch line ................. ~2%,  L≈59
     glass interior ................... L≈13-17, i.e. the world's own value

   R76 drew that as ONE uniform accent stroke plus a 3.6-wide blurred bloom
   ring, which is why it read as neon pipe rather than a machined frame. The
   bands below are those measured proportions mapped onto the existing 100x100
   shell, so the diamond keeps its accepted size, text safe zone, pagination
   and swipe behaviour — only its material changes. Hue still comes from the
   member's theme through the shared plane ladder. */
/* R81 · THE RESPONSE PERIMETER IS A LASER, NOT A FRAME.
   R80 built the rim from a 4.5-unit rail plus a 1.8-unit seat plus two 1.7-unit
   outer chamfers — roughly 30 physical px of banding on a 372px diamond, which
   is why it read as a beveled picture frame. R81 keeps the same square-diamond
   geometry and the same theme ownership but rebuilds the rim as four optical
   layers around a single line:
     1  edge--key      the laser itself: one closed miter-jointed path, a
                       non-scaling hairline, with a two-radius aura that hugs it
     2  bevel--key /   razor chamfers, ~0.7 unit, carrying the material turn on
        bevel--shadow  the upper edges and letting the lower edges recede
     3  rail / catch   a much fainter inset echo, an internal reflection
     4  face / seat    a dark crystal interior seated behind a shadow groove
   Intensity is deliberately uneven: the laser's stroke gradient runs from the
   upper-right key toward the lower-left, so one edge grazes brighter and the
   opposite edge retreats, without any traveling/chase animation. */
